// Originally generated by `npx @oxlint/migrate eslint.config.js --type-aware
// --js-plugins --with-nursery`, then hand-edited. oxlint reads JSONC, so this
// file may carry comments. See dev/Linting.md for the migration's parity notes.
//
// STRUCTURE. The generator emitted one override per eslint flat-config block,
// each spelling out its full rule set — 1262 entries, most of them the same rule
// repeated across eight near-identical blocks, because eslint's `rules` entries
// REPLACE rather than merge. oxlint's overrides do merge, so the blocks below
// are instead grouped by which trees share a rule: each rule appears exactly
// once, in a block whose `files` is the union of the trees that had it. The
// grouping is mechanical and rule-for-rule identical to what it replaced.
//
// Read the `files` list as "these trees agree about the rules in this block".
// The first block is the shared core; the rest get progressively narrower. To
// change one tree's rules, move the rule to a block matching only that tree
// rather than editing a shared block's `files`.
//
// The keys-only blocks after them carry `env` per tree — globals are not rules
// and so have no group to belong to. The blocks after THOSE are the narrow
// exceptions: a rule turned off (or retuned) for one path, with its reason.
//
// Every file oxlint lints must resolve to a non-empty rule map. Nothing is
// hoisted to the top level, so a tree absent from every `files` list is linted
// with zero rules and reports clean — silently. When adding a tree, add it to
// the blocks whose rules it agrees with; `dev/Linting.md` has the check.
//
// The edits the generator cannot make itself are:
//
//  1. jsdoc/require-param loses its `enableFixer` option — oxlint has no such
//     option and only applies fixers under --fix anyway.
//  2. Options are spelled out wherever oxlint's default differs from the eslint
//     rule's, since the generator emits the bare rule name and lets the default
//     stand. Each such entry carries a comment saying which way it differs; see
//     no-eval, no-irregular-whitespace and jsdoc/require-param.
//  3. `ignores` inside an override is dropped by the generator (oxlint has no
//     such field). Where it still mattered it is restored as a LATER override
//     turning the rule back off over those paths — see the no-restricted-globals
//     pair. Two were deliberately not restored, both moot: import/extensions
//     over the Peggy wrappers (they import with an extension, so they satisfy
//     "always" anyway) and max-lines over a file that no longer exists.
//  4. Rules whose oxlint implementation disagrees with the eslint one on this
//     codebase are turned off where they misfire, with the reason at the entry.
//     Most are their own narrow override at the end of the file; one that is
//     off everywhere (sonarjs/assertions-in-tests) sits in its shared block.
//     Re-enable as oxlint fixes them.
//  5. Rules the generator dropped because oxlint's native plugin of the same
//     name lacks them are restored through the JS-plugin bridge — see the
//     unicorn-js/* entries, and dev/Linting.md for the full list. The alias is
//     required: `unicorn` is a reserved native plugin name.
//
// An override that names a rule MUST also list that rule's plugin in its own
// `plugins`/`jsPlugins`, even to turn the rule off — nothing accumulates across
// blocks, and a rule named without its plugin is silently ignored.
//
// Never put a lint directive on line 1 of a file: the JS-plugin bridge is off by
// one on positions, and at line 1 it throws instead of reporting. See
// dev/Linting.md.
